.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-CHANGE-USERID 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.1.37" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-change-userid \- give a user a different user ID
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eam change-userid
\fB\-\-user\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fIUSERID\fR
\fB\-\-new\-user\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fIUSERID\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Renames a user via the eam service's
.I change-userid
action. Requires administrator privileges.
.PP
Both ids are named outright. Unlike
.BR euclid-cli-eam-change-password (1),
which means your own when it names nobody, this one has no "mine" \- a
rename that could mean the caller by saying nothing is one keystroke
from renaming the wrong account.
.SH WHAT MOVES WITH THE NAME
A user ID is not only a label, and three things are built out of it:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
the user's
.BR ERN ,
which is rebuilt from the new id in the account and region it always
named;
.IP \(bu 3
every
.B grant
written to that ERN, which is the whole of what the user may do;
.IP \(bu 3
their
.B membership
of any user group, since a group's members are a list of user ids rather
than of ERNs \- and grants held through a group go with it.
.RE
.PP
All three follow the rename. Without them the user would authenticate
perfectly and hold nothing, which from the outside is indistinguishable
from a permissions bug.
.PP
Access keys travel with the user because they live in the user record,
and keep their ids \- so anything already signing with one goes on
working.
.SH WHAT DOES NOT
The history. An audit record says who did something under the name they
did it under, and rewriting it would be falsifying it. The same goes for
the owner recorded on a bucket, a queue or an object: that names who made
the thing, not who exists now.
.SH REFUSALS
.TP
.B Another user holds the new id
Answered 409. User ids are unique across the installation.
.TP
.B An application runs as this user
Answered 409, naming the application. An application records the identity
it runs as by user id, and the manager looks it up on every reconcile to
hand the process its credentials \- so renaming the user out from under
it leaves the application running and refused everything it calls, with
one line in the manager's log to say so. Point it elsewhere with
.B euclid-cli eap update-application \-\-user
first.
.SH SESSIONS
A session already open is not ended. Its bearer token is a JWT carrying
the old id as its subject and is verified against the signing secret
rather than against anything stored, so it stays valid until it expires
\- and cannot be refreshed after that, because the subject no longer
resolves. Renaming yourself therefore means logging in again under the
new id, and the command says so when it happens.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
The user to rename. Required.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
The id they should have from now on. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The renamed user is printed as JSON, as
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-user (1)
reports one \- so a script can read the new ERN out of the answer rather
than building it.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, not an administrator, no such user, the new id
is taken, an application runs as this user, or the server could not be
reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 eam change-userid \e
    --user jens --new-user jens.vogt
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
